 To solve this, we first perform polynomial division to find the quotient when $x^4 + Px^2 + Q$ is divided by $x^2 + 2x + 5$. We set up the division as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc cc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & + (P-4)x & + (Q-2P+20) \\
\cline{2-5}
x^2 + 2x + 5 & x^4 & + 0x^3 & + (P-5)x^2 & + 0x & + Q \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^4} & + 2x^3 & + 5x^2 \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & - 2x^3 & + (P-10)x^2 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & - 2x^3 & - 4x^2 & - 10x \\
\cline{3-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & (P-6)x^2 & + 10x \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & (P-6)x^2 & + 2(P-6)x & + 5(P-6) \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & (10-2P+12)x & + (5P-30+Q) \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & (10-2P)x & + (10-2P+Q) \\
\cline{5-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 0 & (P+Q-20) \\
\end{array}
\]
Since $x^2 + 2x + 5$ is a factor, the remainder must be zero. Therefore, $P + Q - 20 = 0 \rightarrow P + Q = 20.$</think> <answer>20</answer>

